      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo_Mountain"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Navajo people call this sacred peak Naatsis'aan, which translates to Head of the Earth Woman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://onlineutah.us/navajo_mountain_history.shtml"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hopi descendants call Navajo Mountain Tokonave, or Heart of the Earth, and priests still make pilgrimages to shrines in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo_Mountain"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The summit of Navajo Mountain is the highest point on the Navajo Nation, reaching approximately ten thousand three hundred eighty-eight feet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo_Mountain"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Navajo Mountain beardtongue is a rare plant found mainly on the upper elevation slopes of the mountain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
